<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mindon Min</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as King Mindon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mindon Min was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1853 to 1878, the second-last ruler of the Konbaung dynasty. He took the throne during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-burmese-war">Second Anglo-Burmese War</a> and sought to preserve the remaining kingdom through reform and diplomacy with the British. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> as his capital in 1857, modernised the administration and coinage, and convened the Fifth Buddhist Council in 1871, which had the Pali canon inscribed on marble slabs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mindon-min.jpg" alt="Mindon Min" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ekyaw · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:-Mindon_Min-2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At <b>Mindon</b>’s Kuthodaw Pagoda in Mandalay the entire Pali Buddhist canon is carved on 729 marble slabs, each housed in its own small shrine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1875 an agreement between <b>King Mindon</b> of the Konbaung dynasty and the British recognised the Karenni states as independent of both, a status that made them unlike the Shan states.</blockquote><a href="/state/karenni-states">Karenni States</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarapura">Amarapura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.85,96.1&amp;year=1853" aria-label="Amarapura on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7127 1356 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mindon Min"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7577 1626h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7577 1626h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.85,96.1&amp;year=1853">What was here in 1853? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
